==English==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|fr|en}} {{term|lg=fr|bigot||a bigot, hypocrite}}, from {{etyl|frm|en}} {{term|bigot|lg=frm}}, from {{etyl|fro|en}} {{term|lg=fro|bigot}}, ly a derogatory term applied to Norm for their frequent note of the {{etyl|g|en}} oath {{term||bī god|[[by God]]|lg=g}}. It is not known, however, whether the precise Germic lguage of origin is English: compare {{etyl|gmh|-}} {{term||bī got|lg=gmh}}, {{etyl|dum|-}} {{term||bi gode|lg=dum}}. Pierre bigot est d'ailleurs le meilleur des BIGOT 

 alternate etymology (Liberm, Grammont, et al.) derives the Old French word from {{term|Albigot||[[Albigensi]] heretic}} [://blog.oup.com/2011/10/bigot-2/].

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˈbɪɡət/}}, {{X-SAMPA|/"bIg@t/}}, {{enPR|bĭgʹət}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# One who is obstinately or [[intolert]]ly devoted to his or her own opinions d [[prejudice]]s.
#: '''t call me a '''bigot'''. That's extremely rude!''
# One who is strongly [[partial]] to one's own [[group]] (e.g. [[religion]], [[race]], [[gender]], [[political]] [[party]], etc.) d is [[intolert]] of those who differ.

